ut how she stays inclusive in her work.\n  - posters: Caroline described the posters at her event as amazing, full of pride and strength, which inspired her to make art.\n  - painting: Caroline's painting symbolizes her journey as a trans woman with red and blue, representing breaking rigid thinking and self-love. Melanie wanted the painting to have peaceful blue streaks for tranquility and vibrant colors.\n  - abstract painting: Melanie mentioned an abstract painting she created and invited Caroline to view it, noting art allows for emotional expression.\n  - differences: Caroline made an item that is a symbol of togetherness to celebrate differences and become closer; she wants to make something similar next.\n  - red and blue: Melanie commented that a painting featuring red and blue colors was awesome and asked Caroline what inspired it.\n  - pottery: Melanie recently took a break from pottery due to an injury last month; Caroline offered support. Melanie uses pottery for self-expression and peace, having seven years of experience.\n  - LGBTQ artists: Caroline mentioned an event featuring LGBTQ artists to spread understanding and acceptance.\n  - creative outlets: Caroline asked Melanie if she has any paintings to show related to her creative outlets.\n  - the kids: The kids loved making something with clay, which Melanie described as special to watch their creativity and imagination come to life. Caroline asked what the best part was and if they do it often.\n  - mental health work: Caroline is passionate about pursuing counseling or mental health work to help others and make a positive impact, which she finds rewarding.\n  - a family for kids: Caroline is thrilled to make a family for kids who need one, acknowledging the challenge of being a single parent but accepting the challenge.\n  - Melanie: Melanie congratulated Caroline on passing adoption agency interviews, viewing adoption as a loving way to build a family for needy kids, and encouraged Caroline to be